% describe panels, colours, or a distinguished N. It replaced a three-panel
% colour-coded version deliberately: the referee read the old 10 mK residual
% as a sensitivity claim, and a labelled N = NA in the figure invites the same
% misreading with no caption able to undo it.
%
% Two sentences here are load-bearing and must survive editing:
%   - that the N = NAM1 residual is ABOVE the median retained signal. This is
%     what retires the manuscript's old "eight modes reach 10 mK" claim, and
%     the figure no longer carries a 10 mK line to make the point visually.
%   - that retained RMS is not retained signal SHAPE. The frequency-space
%     panel that used to show this is gone, so the words now carry it alone.
% ===================================================================

\caption{How much of the 21-cm signal lies outside the foreground subspace.
Black: the RMS of the simulated antenna temperature left outside its own
leading $N$ spectral eigenmodes, the quantity previously plotted alone. Grey:
an ensemble of @@NMODELS@@ global 21-cm models decomposed on those identical
modes (5--95 per cent of the ensemble shaded, median dashed), so that the
signal and the foregrounds are read off the same axes. The two cross at
$N=@@NA@@$, where the foregrounds leave @@FG@@\,mK against a median retained
signal of @@MED@@\,mK, or @@KEEPPCT@@ per cent of its band RMS, and @@ABOVE@@
per cent of the ensemble leaves more than the foregrounds do; at
$N=@@NAM1@@$ the foreground residual is still the larger of the two
(@@FGM1@@ against @@MEDM1@@\,mK). What lies outside the subspace is largely
ringing from a truncated smooth basis rather than a residual absorption
trough, so this RMS measures retained amplitude and not a recovered signal
shape; the text quantifies how far the ensemble stays distinguishable. The
decomposition uses no knowledge of the beam or the sky and is in that sense the
most conservative case; instrumental terms enter at
Fig.~\ref{fig:horizon_shift}, which draws the same grey ensemble on the same
axes. See the text.}


