% ===================================================================
% BLOCK 5 -- section "Forward Modelling", after the existing discussion of
% the horizon_shift results ("... Motion up or down produces the largest
% change in antenna temperature ...").
%
% Four paragraphs: (1) a position error is not "more foreground" -- the
% 99.8-per-cent statistic flatters it, and the leakage/compression numbers say
% so; (2) the floor is an in-sample optimum for a KNOWN horizon, and the
% own-basis decomposition shows a displaced sky is no harder to compress, so
% the excess is basis mismatch; (3) the induced floor, its scaling, and the
% requirement that follows; (4) why neither figure is the analysis EIGSEP runs.
%
% Paragraph 2 is the load-bearing one and is what replaced the old "costs one
% more mode" reading. Do not restore that reading: it asserts a difference in
% spectral complexity that the own-basis floors refute.
% ===================================================================

A @@TOPMAG@@\,m displacement changes the antenna temperature by up to
@@MAXDT_E@@\,K to the east, @@MAXDT_N@@\,K to the north and @@MAXDT@@\,K
upward, in each case peaking at the bottom of the band, and @@LEADPCT@@ per
cent of that power lies in the two leading eigenmodes of the unperturbed
antenna temperature. That comparison flatters the result. The foregrounds are
more concentrated still, leaving $@@LEAKFG@@$ of their power outside those two
modes against $@@LEAKE@@$, $@@LEAKN@@$ and $@@LEAKU@@$ for the eastward,
northward and upward displacements; over the first ten modes the foreground
power spectrum falls by @@DECFG@@ decades and the displacement's by @@DECU@@. A
position error is not simply more foreground. It shares the foregrounds'
leading modes but has a shallower spectrum, and so does not compress as they
do: the nominal antenna temperature is @@TANT@@\,K and leaves @@FG@@\,mK
outside a @@NA@@-dimensional subspace, a compression of $@@COMPFG@@$, whereas
a @@TOPMAG@@\,m vertical displacement is @@DTU@@\,K and leaves
@@SYSUPOOL@@\,mK, a compression of @@COMPU@@. Per unit amplitude it is some
three orders of magnitude less compressible than the sky it perturbs, which is
why a perturbation of $T_{\text{ant}}$ at the 0.1 per cent level deposits more
outside the subspace than the whole foreground does.

That floor is moreover a property of a horizon that is known. The eigenmodes
are the right singular vectors of the nominal antenna temperature itself, so
the foregrounds are evaluated in the basis that is optimal for them by
construction, whereas a position error is not. The asymmetry is not a sampling
effect: the waterfall is @@NTIME@@ spectra of @@NFREQ@@ channels, $N=@@NA@@$ is
a small fraction of its @@NRANK@@ available modes, and a basis built from half
the sidereal day describes the other half with no measurable penalty (a floor
ratio of @@CVPEN@@). It is specific to the horizon. Decomposing each displaced sky in its own basis
returns a floor of @@OWNLO@@--@@OWNHI@@\,mK at $N=@@NA@@$ for every
displacement up to @@TOPMAG@@\,m, and @@OWNLO10@@--@@OWNHI10@@\,mK even at
@@MAGHI@@\,m: a displaced antenna temperature is not a more complex object, and
would be described just as economically if the displacement were known.

What a position error costs is therefore the mismatch, and it is that we quote
rather than a mode count. Evaluated in the nominal basis, a @@TOPMAG@@\,m
vertical displacement raises the floor from @@FG@@ to @@FLOORU1@@\,mK, an
eastward one to @@FLOORE1@@\,mK, and a northward one leaves it essentially
unchanged at @@FLOORN1@@\,mK; at @@MAGHI@@\,m the vertical figure is
@@FLOORU10@@\,mK, and at @@MAGLO@@\,m it is @@FLOORU01@@\,mK, an increase of
@@FLOORU01PCT@@ per cent. The vertical response is the one that scales: over
the two decades simulated the residual it leaves at $N=@@NA@@$ is proportional
to the displacement to within @@UPDEV@@ per cent per decade and symmetric in
its sign, because a vertical shift lowers the horizon by a near-uniform offset
(Fig.~\ref{fig:horizon}b), and this is the parallel spacing of the three
magnitude bundles in Fig.~\ref{fig:horizon_shift}. The horizontal responses are
neither linear nor symmetric --- a horizontal shift changes the horizon by an
amount that depends on where the cliff edges fall in azimuth, which is why
those curves in Fig.~\ref{fig:horizon}(b) are spiky, and why the east and north
bundles are not evenly spaced --- but they are also the smaller terms, and the
vertical axis sets the requirement. Holding the power a position error
deposits outside the subspace to a tenth of the median retained signal requires
the antenna's vertical position to be known to roughly @@SPECM@@\,m, and we
adopt that as the position-monitoring requirement.

This also answers the question of why one would not simply remove every mode
the figures display. Doing so does improve the ratio of signal to systematic:
at $N=@@NA@@$ the median retained model and the leftover from a @@TOPMAG@@\,m
vertical error are equal, whereas by $N=@@NEND@@$ the model is @@SIGSYSEND@@
times the larger. But two things go wrong. The signal that survives is
@@KEEPEND@@\,mK, @@KEEPENDPCT@@ per cent of its band RMS, and it is no longer
trough-shaped: the share of the surviving power inside 70--130\,MHz falls from
@@TROUGHNA@@ per cent at $N=@@NA@@$ to @@TROUGHEND@@ per cent at
$N=@@NEND@@$, against @@TROUGHFLAT@@ per cent for a residual with no preference
at all, the remainder being ringing at the band edges. And the foreground
residual that motivates going deeper is the singular-value tail of the matrix
that defined the basis, so its collapse is guaranteed by construction rather
than measured. Tracked against a perturbation that did not help build the
basis, the limiting term changes hands: the foregrounds are the larger of the
two by @@HANDFAC@@ times at $N=@@HANDPREV@@$, the two are equal at $N=@@NA@@$,
and beyond $N=@@HANDSTAY@@$ the @@TOPMAG@@\,m vertical error is consistently
larger, reaching @@HANDEND@@ times the foreground residual by $N=@@NEND@@$.
Past that handover, removing further modes takes signal out of a residual that
is no longer made of foreground. The depth at which to stop is therefore set by
the instrument model rather than by the sky, and it cannot be read off the
foreground curve at all.

The wider point is methodological. An unmodelled displacement of the size we
must anticipate deposits power that resembles the retained signal in both
amplitude and shape --- at $N=@@NA@@$ the leftover from a @@TOPMAG@@\,m
vertical error and the median 21-cm model are equal in RMS, and their cosine
similarity reaches @@COSMAX@@ (median @@COSMED@@). A residual left by a
projection of fixed depth is therefore not evidence of a cosmological signal:
excess with respect to a foreground model is only as trustworthy as the
instrument model behind it, a lesson the field has already drawn from the
scrutiny of published detections
\citep{2018Natur.564E..32H, 2019ApJ...874..153B, 2019ApJ...880...26S}.
Figs.~\ref{fig:singular_values} and~\ref{fig:horizon_shift} should accordingly
be read as measurements of spectral overlap -- how many dimensions the
instrument-weighted foregrounds occupy, and how a position error compares with
the signal inside that subspace -- and not as a proposed pipeline. EIGSEP's
analysis instead forward-models the instrument (this section), with the antenna
position among the parameters that are fitted and marginalised over rather than
assumed. The differentiable forward model makes that marginalisation tractable,
and the position sensitivities computed here are what set the priors it needs.
The same argument applies to every other respect in which the simulated
instrument may differ from the real one; the horizon is the one we have probed.


% ===================================================================
% BLOCK 6 -- section "Forward Modelling". Replaces the sentence beginning
% "To quantify the effect of the horizon coupling, we computed the change in
% antenna temperature ... by 1 m to the east, north, and up ...".
%
% Needed because Fig.~\ref{fig:horizon_shift}'s residual row now shows three
% displacement magnitudes, and only 1 m was previously introduced. No new
% simulations: run_sims.py has always produced +/-0.1, 1 and 10 m for each
% axis (see positions.py), and the other magnitudes were simply unplotted.
% ===================================================================

To quantify the effect of the horizon coupling, we computed the change in
antenna temperature, $\Delta T_{\text{ant}}(\nu)$, caused by displacing the
antenna to the east, north, and up, relative to the nominal position used for
the horizon profile of Fig.~\ref{fig:horizon}. Each axis was displaced by
@@MAGLO@@, @@TOPMAG@@ and @@MAGHI@@\,m, spanning the range from a
displacement we expect to be able to monitor to one large enough to be
obviously unacceptable, so that the dependence on displacement can be read
off rather than inferred from a single case.
